<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><div class="snap"><h2>Augusta at a glance</h2><dl>
<div><dt>County</dt><dd>Kennebec County</dd></div><div><dt>Region</dt><dd>Central Maine &amp; the Kennebec Valley</dd></div>
<div class="wide"><dt>Neighborhoods and villages</dt><dd><ul class="chips"><li>Water Street downtown</li><li>Sand Hill</li><li>the East Side</li><li>Capitol area</li><li>North Augusta</li><li>Hallowell</li><li>Manchester</li></ul></dd></div>
<div class="wide"><dt>Housing mix</dt><dd>Nineteenth-century brick commercial blocks on Water Street, Victorian and Colonial Revival homes near the State House, mill-era duplexes on the East Side, and capes and ranches toward North Augusta and Manchester.</dd></div>
<div class="wide"><dt>Common claim risks</dt><dd><ul class="chips coral"><li>River-adjacent water</li><li>Ice dams</li><li>Windstorm trees</li><li>Frozen pipes</li></ul></dd></div></dl>
<h3>Storms on record</h3><ol class="mini-storms"><li><b>Dec 2023</b><span><strong>December 18 storm.</strong> Kennebec reached major flood stage, swamping Water Street lots in Augusta and downtown Hallowell.</span></li><li><b>Apr 1987</b><span><strong>April 1987 floods.</strong> Kennebec flood inundated low-lying downtown Augusta and Hallowell along the river.</span></li><li><b>Jan 1998</b><span><strong>Ice Storm of 1998.</strong> Ice brought down trees and lines across the Kennebec Valley; outages lasted days to weeks.</span></li></ol></div>
<h2>Augusta claims in context</h2>
<p>Augusta is the capital and the county seat of Kennebec County, and it is a river city first. Water Street’s brick blocks sit right on the Kennebec, the East Side climbs from the river with mill-era duplexes and older single-family homes, and the Capitol area holds Victorian and Colonial Revival houses on tree-lined streets. An insurance appraiser in Augusta has to account for all of these: masonry downtown, plaster and old framing up the hill, and newer construction in North Augusta.</p>
<h2>The Kennebec and the flood line</h2>
<p>Twice in living memory the river has taken over downtown. The April 1987 floods put the Kennebec into low-lying parts of Augusta and Hallowell, and on December 18, 2023, the river reached major flood stage again, filling the Water Street parking lots and flooding businesses in neighboring Hallowell. That storm also brought strong winds that toppled trees across the Kennebec Valley.</p>
<p>River flooding is excluded under homeowners and most commercial property policies, and it belongs to flood insurance. But a storm like December 2023 almost always produces both kinds of damage. A tree through the roof, wind-driven rain through a damaged wall, or a sump failure with its own endorsement may fall under the property policy. Keeping those losses separate and well documented matters before any appraisal begins.</p>
<h2>Ice, outages and frozen pipes</h2>
<p>The Ice Storm of January 1998 is still the benchmark for central Maine outages. When heat is out for days in January, pipes freeze and split, and the water damage that follows can dwarf the tree damage that started it. The same pattern repeats on a smaller scale nearly every winter. If you own a camp or second home on one of the area’s lakes, our <a href="/claim-math/seasonal-camp-vacancy-check/">seasonal camp vacancy check</a> helps you see how vacancy and freeze-protection terms in your policy may apply.</p>
<h2>Where an Augusta insurance appraiser looks closer</h2>
<ul>
<li>Brick and masonry work on Water Street buildings, including scaffolding and street access.</li>
<li>Hidden water in plaster walls and ceilings after a freeze.</li>
<li>Tree removal costs on steep East Side lots where cranes are needed.</li>
<li>Contents and inventory for downtown businesses.</li>
</ul>
<p>Commercial owners on Water Street face an extra layer: many buildings share party walls, so a fire or roof leak in one block can damage its neighbors. Business income, inventory and tenant improvements all come into play. Homeowners in Sand Hill and on the East Side often have older plumbing and wiring that repairs expose, and code upgrades may be covered if the policy includes ordinance or law coverage.</p>
<p>Appraisal is designed for disputes over the amount of loss. Our <a href="/the-appraisal-path/">appraisal process guide</a> explains each step, and the <a href="/claim-math/claim-date-tracker/">claim date tracker</a> helps you keep proof-of-loss and policy dates in view. For coverage disputes or questions about legal deadlines, consult a Maine attorney.</p>

<h2>Appraisal work for Augusta properties</h2>
<p>Every Maine Insurance Appraiser service is available in Augusta and the surrounding towns, including:</p>
<ul><li><a href="/appraisal-work/homeowner-dispute-appraisal/">Homeowner Claim Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-work/noreaster-hurricane-appraisal/">Nor’easter &amp; Hurricane Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-work/water-damage-loss-appraisal/">Water Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-work/ice-dam-snow-load-appraisal/">Ice Dam &amp; Snow Load Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-work/fallen-tree-limb-appraisal/">Fallen Tree Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-work/carrier-estimate-line-review/">Insurance Estimate Review</a></li></ul>
<h2>Augusta questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>My Augusta business had river water and roof damage in December 2023. Can appraisal handle both?</summary><p>Appraisal sets the amount of a covered loss. River flood damage is usually excluded and handled by flood insurance, while wind and roof damage may be covered by the property policy, so the losses should be documented separately.</p></details><details><summary>Do frozen pipes after a power outage count as a covered loss?</summary><p>Often yes, but policies can limit coverage if heat was not maintained or the home was vacant. Read the freeze and vacancy language in your policy, and keep records of the outage.</p></details><details><summary>Can I invoke appraisal if the carrier has not finished its estimate?</summary><p>Generally appraisal is used once there is a disagreement over the amount of loss. Check your policy’s appraisal clause for its exact conditions and timing, and ask a Maine attorney about deadlines.</p></details></div>
